      "passage_as_proposed": "During the course of the day, if a significant heat load is experienced and the maternal core temperature increases beyond 38.5 o C (see below) the guidelines on treatment of heat strain will be followed with some additional considerations. In non‑pregnant individuals, heat strain is determined to be a life‑threatening emergency requiring immediate treatment when core temperature reaches 40.5°C. In pregnancy, heat is known to be teratogenic in the embryonic period, and throughout pregnancy compensatory mechanisms may be compromised. Hence we do not think it is ethical to allow the temperature to reach such a high level. Therefore, should maternal core temperature reach 38.5°C, this would result in an immediate review and treatment of heat strain would be commenced.",
